<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sweet-potato">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sweet potato</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sweet potatoes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> is a starchy root crop native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> that spread across the world after the sixteenth century through Spanish and Portuguese trade networks. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> it was one of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">New World crops</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, that allowed cultivation of marginal hill and upland land. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> link these crops to the dramatic population growth of Qing China.</p></div>
